From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 11:12:32 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: give back garbage at a refusal, and idle storage only

When the host driver refused a resource's storage, the renderer gave
back what every context held for completed work and everything the host
drivers cached, then made the resource once more. A context that is
still drawing frees its batch states and command storage at every
submission and takes them again for the next frame, so the reclaim freed
storage that context allocated again at once, outside any budget: the
retried resource took the room, the storage came back past it, and an
Android guest drawing WebGL at the full budget went past it by about
100 MiB.

What a reclaim frees is now split by what taking it again costs. Every
GL context is trimmed, so the driver drops the references its completed
batches keep to resources the guest has released; that memory comes
back only through an allocation the budget gates. The host drivers'
cached buffers are freed at any age for the same reason. Command storage
is not gated, so it goes only once unused for a while
(VKR_CACHED_MEMORY_IDLE_NS for the Vulkan devices).

virgl_renderer_set_context_quiet_query() gives the renderer the host's
word on which contexts are quiet, nothing having run on them for a while
and no fence of theirs outstanding. Only those are flushed and finished
before the trim: a flush would submit a drawing context's frame early
and a finish would stall it. The shared-memory segments whose fences
have signalled are freed. Without a query every context is finished, as
before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
